WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.180
ORI KASHI: Welcome.

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:03.630
My name is Ori Kashi,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.410
on the Cloud SQL team.

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:09.090
In this video, I'll show you
how to create, run, and manage

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:12.030
SQL instances using
Google Cloud SQL.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.030
Cloud SQL helps manage
mundane administrative tasks

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.660
for MySQL, Postgre, and
Microsoft SQL Server

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.610
databases so you can
focus on what matters.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.330
Let's walk through how to create
a SQL Server database on Cloud

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:28.560
SQL and connect to it from
SQL Server Management Studio.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.038
You can set up an instance
in just a few steps.

00:00:31.038 --> 00:00:32.580
Let's go through
the basics, and I'll

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.890
point out some options for
additional configuration

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:35.770
along the way.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.700
We'll start by navigating to
Cloud SQL in the GCP console

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.125
and clicking Create Instance.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.780
This instance will
be created in the US,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.360
and we'll leave
the zone set to any

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.520
so Cloud SQL can choose for us.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.370
Now let's take a look at
our configuration options.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.400
You can specify your
addition, the instance size,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.490
high availability, and more.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:05.890
For this demo, I'll stick
with Standard Edition,

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.300
and I'll choose a four
core high memory instance.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.708
Maximizing memory is a
good choice for SQL Server

00:01:11.708 --> 00:01:13.750
because it can take
advantage of the extra memory

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.460
for performance.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.340
That said, don't worry too
much about initial sizing.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.640
You can scale the
instance up or down later.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.490
Let's increase the storage
capacity to 200 gigabytes

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.177
because increasing disk capacity
also increases performance.

00:01:28.177 --> 00:01:30.260
Check out the performance
calculator on the right.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.350
As I add capacity,
my disk performance

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.120
increases, which
allows my database

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.860
to process more
operations per second.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.960
I'll leave Automatic
Storage increases

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.030
enabled so that I
don't accidentally

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.280
run out of disk space.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.670
Lastly, let's ensure that the
database will automatically

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.650
provide isolation from failures.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.810
We can do that by enabling
the high availability

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.210
configuration,
which for SQL Server

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:58.370
adds a standby instance
in a different zone.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.950
Now that we're happy with the
configuration of our instance,

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.200
let's kick off the creation.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.450
It may take a few minutes
for your instance to create.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.092
This is a great time to
grab a quick cup of coffee.

00:02:07.092 --> 00:02:09.009
You can also check the
status of your instance

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:11.420
by navigating to
your instance list.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.073
Once you see a green checkmark,
your instance is ready.

00:02:15.073 --> 00:02:16.990
Looks like our instance
has finished creating.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.900
Let's open the Details
page to continue.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.960
There are many ways to
connect to this instance.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.910
Within GCP, for
example, we could

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.957
use App Engine, Compute
Engine, or Container Engine,

00:02:28.957 --> 00:02:31.040
but we could also connect
from just about anywhere

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.240
by authorizing IP addresses
or using the Cloud SQL proxy.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.330
For this demo, I'll connect
using a familiar tool, SQL

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.250
Server Management Studio.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.850
I'll be running SQL Server
Management Studio from a GCEVM,

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:46.220
and so I'm able to connect
securely and easily

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.150
via private IP.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.250
Once I remote
desktop into my VM,

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.450
I'll start SSMS and then put in
the private IP of my Cloud SQL

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.770
instance.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.100
I'm using the default
user SQL Server,

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.460
and then I enter our root
password from before.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.277
And now we're connected,
so let's go ahead

00:03:06.277 --> 00:03:07.610
and create and insert some data.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:14.930
To begin, I'll
create a database.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.760
Next, I'll insert some data.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.340
Finally, let's make sure the
data is now in our database.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.010
And it is.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.240
Great.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.510
So to recap, we just
created a Cloud SQL instance

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.000
with high performance
and high availability

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.270
and connected to it with SQL
Server Management Studio.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.050
Now that you've seen how to
get started, give it a try.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.550
You can learn more about Google
Cloud SQL and our free trial

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:47.670
program at
Cloud.Google.com/free.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:48.830
Thank you for watching.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:52.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

